    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L08-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "8.4: Solve These Equations With Cube Roots in Them",
      "SourceExcerpt": "All of these equations were equivalent to equations that could be written in the form \\(\\sqrt[3]{ax+b}+c=0\\) for some constants \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\). Find a formula that would solve any such equation for \\(x\\) in terms of \\(a\\), \\(b\\),…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L08-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "8.4: Solve These Equations With Cube Roots in Them",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Every number has exactly one cube root. You can see this by looking at the graph of \\(y=x^3\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L08-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "8.4: Solve These Equations With Cube Roots in Them",
      "SourceExcerpt": "If \\(y\\) is any number, for example, -4, then we can see that \\(y= \\text{-}4\\) crosses the graph in one and only one place, so the equation \\(x^3 = \\text{-}4\\) will have the solution \\(\\text{-}\\sqrt[3]{4}\\). This is true for any number…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L08-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "8.4: Solve These Equations With Cube Roots in Them",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In an equation like \\(\\sqrt[3]{t} + 6 = 0\\), we can isolate the cube root and cube each side:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L08-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "8.4: Solve These Equations With Cube Roots in Them",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\begin{align}\\sqrt[3]{t} + 6 &= 0\\\\ \\sqrt[3]{t} &= \\text{-}6\\\\ t &= (\\text{-}6)^3 \\\\ t &= \\text{-}216 \\end{align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L08-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "8.4: Solve These Equations With Cube Roots in Them",
      "SourceExcerpt": "While cubing each side of an equation won’t create an equation with solutions that are different than the original equation, it is still a good idea to always check solutions in the original equation because little mistakes can creep in…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
